# FantasyDesk — Caddy reverse proxy / static file server.
#
# Why Caddy instead of nginx+certbot: Caddy issues and renews Let's
# Encrypt (and, as a fallback, ZeroSSL) certificates automatically the
# first time it sees a request for a domain listed below — no separate
# certbot install, no cron job to renew certs, no manual nginx vhost +
# certbot --nginx dance, and no risk of a forgotten renewal silently
# expiring a cert on a box nobody is watching full-time. For a small
# self-hosted deployment with a handful of subdomains, that's the entire
# reverse-proxy + TLS story in the ~20 lines below, with no extra moving
# parts to operate.
#
# Domain values come from docker-compose.yml's environment (which reads
# them from .env — see .env.example). Caddy substitutes {$VAR} from its
# own process environment, same mechanism .env variables reach it through
# compose's `environment:`/`env_file:` on the caddy service.
#
# Internal services are reached by their Docker Compose service name on the
# shared network (Docker's embedded DNS resolves "web-next", "mobile-api", etc.
# to the right container) — none of those ports are published to the host,
# only Caddy's 80/443 are, so port 8080 behind each name is never directly
# reachable from outside the box.
{
	email {$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL}
}

# Shared response compression — applied to the proxied app/API responses and
# the static marketing site below. zstd is tried first (better ratio/speed
# than gzip when the client supports it, e.g. recent Chrome/Firefox/curl),
# falling back to gzip for older clients; Caddy negotiates via the request's
# Accept-Encoding header, so clients that send neither get an uncompressed
# response same as today. Not applied to the two webhook endpoints — those
# are small, infrequent, machine-to-machine payloads where compression has
# no measurable upside.
(encode_responses) {
	encode zstd gzip
}

# --- fantasygm-lab (web app: Next.js) ----------------------------------------
# The legacy Streamlit app (`web` service, app.py) was removed in 2026-10 --
# see docs/streamlit-removal-2026-10.md. {$APP_DOMAIN} now proxies to the
# Next.js frontend (web-next), same upstream as {$WEB_NEXT_DOMAIN} below.
{$APP_DOMAIN} {
	import encode_responses
	reverse_proxy web-next:8080
}

# --- fantasygm-lab-marketing-www --------------------------------------------
# `www` used to be served the same static marketing page as the apex domain
# (see docs/production-domain-cutover.md's original "canonicalize to apex"
# plan — never implemented as an actual redirect). In practice, users treat
# `www.<domain>` as the product itself, not a marketing mirror: a static
# page with no auth/session awareness looked indistinguishable from "you're
# logged out" even to an already-signed-in user, since session state lives
# only on APP_DOMAIN's origin (localStorage is origin-scoped — see that doc).
# Redirecting straight into the app, preserving path/query, removes that
# confusion entirely: `www` now always reflects the visitor's real auth
# state instead of a static page that can never know it.
{$MARKETING_DOMAIN_WWW} {
	redir https://{$APP_DOMAIN}{uri} permanent
}

# --- fantasygm-lab-marketing (static landing site) --------------------------
# Static files only (no Python process) — same content Render's
# fantasygm-lab-marketing static service publishes from staticPublishPath
# ./static/landing after running scripts/build_static_landing_assets.py.
# That script is idempotent and only touches checked-in assets, so the
# repo's checked-in static/landing/ is served as-is; re-run the script
# after pulling marketing asset changes to refresh it before the next
# `docker compose up -d`.
{$MARKETING_DOMAIN} {
	import encode_responses
	root * /srv/static/landing

	# Filenames here are NOT content-hashed/fingerprinted (checked-in as
	# plain names like landing.css, favicon.png — see
	# scripts/build_static_landing_assets.py), so a long, unconditional
	# max-age would risk serving a stale asset after a deploy until it
	# expires. must-revalidate keeps every cache honest past max-age
	# instead of serving stale-while-offline. Images/fonts change rarely
	# once published, so they get a longer window than CSS/JS, which are
	# more likely to change on a given deploy.
	@static_long path *.jpg *.jpeg *.png *.gif *.webp *.svg *.ico *.woff *.woff2 *.ttf *.eot
	header @static_long Cache-Control "public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate"

	@static_short path *.css *.js
	header @static_short Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate"

	# index.html, robots.txt, sitemap.xml intentionally get no
	# Cache-Control here — they're the files most likely to change on a
	# deploy and aren't fingerprinted, so they fall back to normal
	# conditional revalidation rather than being cached outright.
	header /* {
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
	}
	file_server
}

# --- fantasygm-lab-web-next (Next.js frontend, test-subdomain) -------------
# A separate subdomain that serves the same web-next upstream as
# {$APP_DOMAIN} above (kept so existing links/smoke tests keep working) --
# see docs/SELF_HOSTED_MIGRATION.md's "web-next test-subdomain preview"
# section.
#
# IMPORTANT: production's actual, live Caddy config is generated
# server-side by /usr/local/bin/fantasygmlab-sync-caddy (Cloudflare-Tunnel-
# adapted — see docs/SELF_HOSTED_MIGRATION.md section 5.7), not this
# checked-in file directly. Whether that generator mirrors every block in
# this file automatically, or needs its own update to pick up this new
# route, is a production-box detail outside this repo — confirm on the box
# before assuming this change alone exposes the new subdomain.
{$WEB_NEXT_DOMAIN} {
	import encode_responses
	reverse_proxy web-next:8080
}

# --- fantasydesk-app (new primary domain, parallel with fantasygmlab.com) ---
# Zach, 2026-10-17: "we are switching the landing page and web app to
# fantasydesk.app" — run in parallel with fantasygmlab.com first, verify,
# redirect old->new later (not a hard cutover, see
# project memory "FantasyGMLAB -> FantasyDesk rebrand"). Unlike
# fantasygmlab.com's apex (static marketing landing page) vs. app.
# subdomain (the real app) split, fantasydesk.app's apex routes STRAIGHT
# to web-next — this domain's whole purpose is to BE the app, not host a
# separate marketing site. www redirects to the apex, same pattern
# MARKETING_DOMAIN_WWW uses above.
#
# Explicit http:// prefix (unlike the {$VAR}-driven blocks above, which
# get it auto-added by fantasygmlab-sync-caddy's env-var substitution) —
# a literal domain here isn't a {$VAR} hostname, so the generator never
# touches it; without this prefix Caddy's own auto-HTTPS inferred scheme
# produced a broken/duplicate route on the Cloudflare Tunnel side
# (confirmed via the box's tunnel config, 2026-10-17).
http://fantasydesk.app {
	import encode_responses
	reverse_proxy web-next:8080
}

http://www.fantasydesk.app {
	redir https://fantasydesk.app{uri} permanent
}

# --- fantasygm-lab-mobile-api ------------------------------------------------
{$MOBILE_API_DOMAIN} {
	import encode_responses
	reverse_proxy mobile-api:8080
}

# --- fantasygm-lab-stripe-webhook --------------------------------------------
{$STRIPE_WEBHOOK_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy stripe-webhook:8080
}

# --- fantasygm-lab-revenuecat-webhook -----------------------------------------
{$REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy revenuecat-webhook:8080
}
